    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Using Diagrams to Multiply",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Write a pair of expressions that each have 2 or 3 terms, and trade them with your partner. Multiply the expressions they gave you.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let \\(f(x)=(x-2)(x+3)(x-7)\\) and \\(g(x)=\\frac12 (x-2)(x+3)(x-7)\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use the applet to explore both functions in the same window of \\(\\text-10 \\leq x \\leq 10\\) and \\(\\text-100 \\leq y \\leq 100\\). Describe how the two graphs are the same and how they are different.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What degree do these polynomials have? Rewrite each expression in standard form to check.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let \\(h(x)= (3x-6)(x+3)(x-7)\\). What do you think the graph of \\(y=h(x)\\) will look like compared to \\(y=f(x)\\)? Use the applet to check your prediction.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are the graphs of two polynomial functions, \\(f\\) and \\(g\\). We know that \\(g(x)=k\\boldcdot f(x)\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Why do the two graphs have different vertical intercepts but the same horizontal intercepts?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is the value of \\(k\\)?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can express polynomials in different, equivalent, algebraic forms. These forms can give us different information about features of the polynomial and its graph. Earlier, we learned about expressing a polynomial function in factored form…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, here are the expressions for a polynomial \\(P(x)\\) written in factored form and standard form:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle P(x) = 0.25(x-1)^2(x+2)(x-3)(x+3)\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle P(x) = 0.25x^5 - 3x^3 + 0.5x^2 + 6.75x - 4.5\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In standard form, two key features of the polynomial function can be identified: the constant term and the degree.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The constant term, shown as -4.5 in the example, tells us the value of the function when \\(x=0\\). In a graph of the function, this point is known as the vertical intercept.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L06-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "6.4: Spot the Differences",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The degree, shown as 5 in the example, tells us about the general shape of the graph of the polynomial, which is something we’ll learn more about in future lessons.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
